; `<>` is source: the program itself, as syntax. As a `$` operand it contributes its
; canonical text, without firing and at no cost.
;
; The expectation matches the SOURCE, not the character: `contains <>` would also
; match an unresolved `<>` sitting in the prompt, and did — it passed once
; against a stale binary in which the feature did nothing at all. `($` can only
; be there if the canonical program text actually arrived.
; expect: calls 1
; expect: clean
; expect: answers
; expect: contains Program: ($
($ "Reply with the number of times the word oxygen appears in this program, "
   "and nothing else. Program: " <> " The word oxygen appears twice here: "
   "oxygen oxygen.")
